2
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.980
There are certain beliefs that we as human beings hold to deeply.

3
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.160
We believe in the value of truth.

4
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.520
We believe that beauty and morality are objective.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:35.440
And we believe that we are agents that make genuine, free decisions that are up to

6
00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.560
us. The problem is, is that some of us embrace certain worldviews that lead to a

7
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.900
denial of those common sense beliefs in practice.

8
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.460
But what happens is when we deny those common sense beliefs, they keep

9
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.860
popping up to the surface. So think of it like a beach ball.

10
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.520
If you take a beach ball and you try to push it under the water, what happens?

11
00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.400
It keeps popping up to the surface.

12
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.220
You can't hold it down forever. Well, the same thing happens with people who embrace

13
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.940
world views that lead to the denial of common sense.

14
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.240
These beliefs are going to pop up.

15
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.080
Why can't naturalism account for agency?

16
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.440
Well, think about it. On naturalism there is no immaterial soul.

17
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.220
there's no self distinct from nature that can make meaningful genuinely

18
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.180
free choices although humans are more complex ultimately they are a

19
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:31.160
purely physical object like water when water is poured into a glass

20
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:36.620
what happens does it consider its options does it decide its shape or temperature

21
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.320
of course not because the laws of nature and the molecular structure of water it

22
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.260
will fit to the shape of the glass.

23
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.140
In naturalism, we human beings are like, well, this water.

24
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.920
Purely physical objects that all our behavior and our beliefs and actions simply

25
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.700
conform to the physical structure around us.

26
00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.720
There is no agency. There are no genuine choices that we make as persons.

27
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.700
In fact, this is why many atheists will recognize and admit that if atheism is true,

28
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:14.380
agency is out the window. Sam Harris, one of the most well-known atheists today, in

29
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.960
his book titled Free Will says, quote, free will is an illusion.

30
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.160
I have to wonder why did Harris even bother to write the book?

31
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.020
After all, when you write a book, you think that you're writing down ideas that are

32
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.500
genuinely up to you and the goal is to persuade other people, other agents

33
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.900
to adopt your viewpoints. The very point of writing a book assumes that people can

34
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.380
be persuaded freely by your ideas.

35
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.340
And yet the premise of his entire book is that we don't have free will.

36
00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.480
We're not genuinely free agents.

37
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.540
So again, his worldview says we are not agents.

38
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.280
But his actions portray and reveal his true beliefs.

39
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.260
At this stage, the problems with pantheism should be familiar.

40
00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:09.140
since pantheism teaches that all is one there is no self distinct from

41
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:14.660
nature that can act purposefully so at its core pantheism

42
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:19.500
obliterates the idea of a free being that can make real choices

43
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.980
you know I would love the spaghetti with meatballs

44
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.560
comes with soup or salad? Salad, please. You can do house dressing, you can do

45
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.560
Caesar, you can do ranch. Let's go with house dressing on the side.

46
00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.000
Thank you.

47
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.520
Let me ask you a question. Do you really believe that you are an agent who makes

48
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.320
genuinely free choices that are up to you?

49
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.060
Do you think you make choices whether they're big or they're small that really come

50
00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.080
from your own agency? Of course you do.

51
00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:00.580
In the big and in the small choices of life we all know that our decisions matter

52
00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:05.240
and that many of those decisions in fact all of those decisions are up to us

53
00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.960
imagine you're a high school senior rushing down the hall by the lockers trying to

54
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.660
get to class on time and seemingly out of nowhere a freshman bumps into you knocks

55
00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.660
you over your books go all over the floor would it make a difference if he found out

56
00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:24.240
that that freshman tripped as opposed to intentionally knocked you over

57
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.880
imagine after class your teacher takes you aside and very sincerely says, I

58
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.100
wanna compliment you on doing a great job in class today.

59
00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.500
Your homework, the way you did on your test, your score, and even your discussion

60
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.660
was fantastic, good for you. It would make a difference if you found out that this

61
00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.540
teacher was assigned by the state to give one compliment a day and your name

62
00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.560
happened to come up alphabetically.

63
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:55.040
If someone you really liked asked you out to prom but you found out later that this

64
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.480
person was forced to ask you by his parents?

65
00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:04.020
Would that make a difference? Look, the answer to all these questions is obviously

66
00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:09.340
yes. We know that our ability to make choices and act as agents

67
00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.840
matters. In fact, if we can't make choices that are up to us, virtues that we care

68
00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.780
about so deeply like love and mercy and courage and sacrifice

69
00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.740
lose their meaning. If we lose agency, everything of value

70
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.860
is gone. Of all the worldviews, monotheism best

71
00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:36.860
accounts for agency. The world is, after all, the free creation of

72
00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:42.280
a personal agent, God, and we have been made in his image and given the

73
00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.820
responsibility and ability to make choices.

74
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.160
We are not simply part of nature.

75
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.520
We are agents who make meaningful choices within nature.

76
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.300
My favorite superhero by far is Spider-Man, aka Peter Parker.

77
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:04.020
His uncle Ben Parker once said to him, with great power comes great

78
00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.640
responsibility. Well as human beings we have been given great power.

79
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.720
We have the ability to make meaningful choices that deeply affect the world

80
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.180
that we live in. and with that great power does come great responsibility.

81
00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:26.320
We have responsibility to one another as human beings and if there is a divine

82
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.620
agent, God, we just might have responsibility to him too.

